# CODEOWNERS — baseline template
#
# HOW TO USE THIS FILE
#   1. Copy it to exactly ONE of the locations your forge reads: the repository root,
#      or the forge's config directory, or docs/. Never two — most forges read only one,
#      and the other silently becomes dead config that nobody notices is dead.
#   2. Keep the [BASELINE] rule. Delete every [OPTIONAL] rule you do not need.
#   3. Keep the owning group on every rule you keep. That is the whole standard.
#   4. Verify on a live OPEN pull request, not by re-reading this file. Query the
#      request's approval state and confirm every code-owner rule shows a non-empty
#      eligible-approvers list.
#
#      NOT on the request that adds this file. Forges evaluate CODEOWNERS from the
#      TARGET branch, so on a first-adoption change the target branch has no owners file
#      yet and the approval state reports no code-owner rule at all. An empty result
#      there means NOT YET VERIFIABLE — neither a pass nor a defect. Merge the adoption
#      change first, then check the approval state on the NEXT open request in the repo.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# THE ONE REQUIREMENT: every rule names the owning group.
#
# Individual owners are ADDITIVE alongside the group, never instead of it. Owners within a
# single rule are OR, not AND: a three-owner rule yields ONE approval rule whose eligible
# approvers are the union of the named individuals and the group's members, with a single
# required count. Naming the group only ever WIDENS who may approve. It never adds a
# second required approval.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY EVERY RULE, AND NOT JUST THE CATCH-ALL
#
# Only the LAST matching pattern applies. Matches are NOT unioned.
#
# A change touching a single file under infrastructure/terraform/ matches both `*` and
# `*.tf`, and the approval state returns exactly ONE code-owner rule — `*.tf`. The `*`
# rule does not appear at all.
#
# So the catch-all below protects only the paths that NO other rule matches. Every rule you
# add takes its paths out of the catch-all's coverage and becomes their sole gate. Adding a
# path rule without the group therefore REMOVES protection rather than adding it.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHY A GROUP, AND NOT A PERSON
#
# The author of a change is removed from the eligible approvers — the forge drops them
# rather than rejecting an approval attempt. So a rule whose only owner is one individual
# reports zero required approvals on any change that person authors. It does not block. It
# silently disappears from the gate while still rendering as a named rule in the approval
# state, which is why this class of defect survives code review.
#
# The observed failure: a `*.tf` rule naming one individual, on a change to the terraform
# that deploys staging and production, reported required=0 / approved=true, and no
# code-owner review was ever required.
#
# A group cannot fail that way: it has several members, so at least one non-author owner
# always remains.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# A HANDLE THAT RESOLVES IS NOT AN OWNER
#
# An owner must be a member of THE PROJECT. A handle naming a real, active user who is not
# a project member contributes nothing, and the forge reports no error — the rule simply
# has no eligible approvers, so it requires nothing.
#
# This bites hardest where one person holds two accounts with the same display name: the
# personal one is not a member, the org one is, and rules across many repos name the wrong
# one. Check a handle with a PROJECT MEMBERSHIP query, never with username resolution.
#
# The same applies to GROUPS: a group outside the project's own namespace must be
# explicitly shared with the project, or it confers nothing.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# DO NOT USE SECTION SYNTAX ([Backend], ^[Optional][2]) unless your team already has a
# working example. Sections carry their own approvals count and an optional marker, which
# changes every piece of per-rule reasoning above.
# =======================================================================================


# ── [BASELINE] — required in every repo ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Keep this even if you add nothing else. One group-owned catch-all is the shape that
# cannot fail in either direction. If this is the ONLY rule in the file, the file is
# correct and complete.
#
# It must come FIRST: general-to-specific is the convention that reads correctly against
# last-match-wins.

*                                       @your-org/your-review-group


# ── [OPTIONAL] Add a rule ONLY to give a path EXTRA owners ─────────────────────────────
#
# A rule that names the same owners as the catch-all is pure liability: it changes nothing,
# and it is one more line that can lose the group in a later edit. Add a path rule only
# when a specific reviewer must see that path.
#
# Every example below names an extra, path-specific group AND keeps the owning group.
# Replace the extra group with the one that must see that path, or delete the line.

# --- Deploy-bearing files. Infrastructure and CI config are the highest-blast-radius
# --- paths in every project shape, which is why the failure above happened on exactly these.
# *.tf                                  @your-org/infra-group @your-org/your-review-group
# *.tfvars                              @your-org/infra-group @your-org/your-review-group
# <ci-config-path>                      @your-org/infra-group @your-org/your-review-group
#   ^ your forge's pipeline definition — a top-level CI file, or the directory holding
#     workflow definitions. Use whichever path this repo actually has.
# Dockerfile*                           @your-org/infra-group @your-org/your-review-group

# --- Backend service.
# src/                                  @your-org/backend-group @your-org/your-review-group
# tests/                                @your-org/backend-group @your-org/your-review-group

# --- API and shared libraries.
# database/                             @your-org/api-group @your-org/your-review-group
# shared_libs/                          @your-org/api-group @your-org/your-review-group

# --- Frontend application.
# src/app/                              @your-org/frontend-group @your-org/your-review-group
# next.config.ts                        @your-org/frontend-group @your-org/your-review-group
# package.json                          @your-org/frontend-group @your-org/your-review-group

# --- Monorepo with submodules and a docs site.
# apps/                                 @your-org/platform-group @your-org/your-review-group
# content/                              @your-org/platform-group @your-org/your-review-group
# .gitmodules                           @your-org/platform-group @your-org/your-review-group
# vendor/                               @your-org/platform-group @your-org/your-review-group
#   NOTE a submodule is a gitlink: a directory-suffixed pattern targets files WITHIN the
#   directory, and a submodule contributes none, so `vendor/<submodule>/` may match nothing
#   at all. Settle it with an open request that bumps the submodule pointer.

# --- CI component / template library.
# templates/                            @your-org/ci-group @your-org/your-review-group
# scripts/                              @your-org/ci-group @your-org/your-review-group

# --- Agent plugin / marketplace.
# .claude-plugin/                       @your-org/plugin-maintainers @your-org/your-review-group
# plugins/                              @your-org/plugin-maintainers @your-org/your-review-group
